From 91d8531b077791edbcb050c9a8b344f8ea9928e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: J P <jpwatters@mac.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:11:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 18/41] FT8/FT4 over the NAS: the relay times the transmit
 burst itself

Over the network every tone change crossed Wi-Fi on its own, 160 ms apart.
In 20 test bursts on 2026-10-14 two were held up for 1.1-1.5 s (once by the
Tab5's Wi-Fi SDIO link draining, once in transit), so symbols reached the
radio late while the Tab5's own timing looked perfect.

Now, when the relay announces "txsched=1" in its HELLO, the Tab5 sends the
whole burst in one QMXR/1 TX_SCHED frame at key-down (u8 ver, u8 flags,
u16 n, u32 symbol_us, n x u32 centi-Hz). The relay keys the QMX, sends each
tone on the NAS's clock, sends the key-up tone, and answers TX_DONE (status,
tones sent, RF on-air ms, latest symbol ms). TX_ABORT stops it at once. The
Tab5 still runs its symbol loop for the UI, live power/SWR, SWR protection
and abort, and still sends the post-burst PC;SW; and RX;. If the hand-off
fails it sends the tones itself as before; if no TX_DONE arrives it sends
the key-up tone itself. If the Tab5 disconnects mid-burst the relay keys up
and sends RX;.

A flag file tab5-timed-tx in the relay folder leaves txsched out of the
HELLO, for A/B comparison. The txlog records "tones timed by relay|Tab5",
the CAT log shows relay-sent commands as RELAY>QMX, and tx_compare.py
reports the two modes separately.

Tested: 10 of 10 FT8 bursts through the NAS, relay RF on-air 12640-12641
ms, longest tone gap 161 ms, latest symbol +2 ms, no stalls.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01W7MAsxUnuCnfwkHxdqp61S
